a sickness throughout the army roused, an evil one, and the people were perishing,
because that Chryses he dishonoured, the praying-priest,
Atreus’-son;

“Sons of Atreus and you other well-greaved Achaeans,
to you may the gods grant, who hold the Olympian halls,
to sack-utterly Priam’s city, and well homeward to come;

“Let me not, old man, by the hollow ships find you,
either now lingering or later back again coming,
lest then the sceptre and the fillet of the god not avail you;

sooner old age even will come upon her
in our house, in Argos, far from her fatherland,
at the loom going-to-and-fro and my bed sharing;

So he spoke, praying, and him heard Phoebus Apollo,
and he went down from the peaks of Olympus, incensed in heart,
the bow on his shoulders having and the quiver covered-at-both-ends;

The mules first he went against, and the swift dogs,
but then at the men themselves a shaft, sharp-pointed, letting fly
he struck;

“Son of Atreus, now we, driven-back-off-course, I think,
back home will return, if indeed death at least we escape,
if truly together both war subdues and plague the Achaeans;

for even if his anger at least on that very day he digests,
yet also afterward he keeps resentment, until he fulfils it,
in his own breast;

“Not then over a vow does he find fault, nor a hecatomb,
but because of the praying-priest, whom dishonoured Agamemnon,
and did not release his daughter and did not accept the ransom;
for that reason, then, pains has given the far-shooter, and still will give;
